feature_map:
{\color{blue}For a fixed RBF bandwidth, landmarks are selected using fitting inputs only. The fixed-setting Adam study uses a pool of at most 2,000 source inputs, while the validation-selected study combines up to 2,000 source and 2,000 target fitting inputs. A target-only model uses only its target fitting inputs. If the pool exceeds the requested landmark count, K-means selects that many centers \citep{zhang2010clustered}; otherwise all pool inputs are retained without clustering. The following formulas use $m$ for the resulting number of landmarks. The calling experiment fixes the random state before subset selection; the validation runner records that seed and the selected landmarks, which remain fixed during optimization. Let $\boldsymbol{W}$ be their Gram matrix and $\boldsymbol{k}_Z(\boldsymbol{x})=[k(\boldsymbol{x},z_1),\ldots,k(\boldsymbol{x},z_m)]^T$. A stabilized feature map is}
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:nystrom_feature}
{\color{blue}\psi(\boldsymbol{x})=(\boldsymbol{W}+\varepsilon\boldsymbol{I})^{-1/2}\boldsymbol{k}_Z(\boldsymbol{x}),\qquad \varepsilon=10^{-6}.}
\end{equation}
{\color{blue}The symmetric inverse square root is formed by SVD or symmetric eigendecomposition. The resulting fixed kernel approximation is $\widetilde{k}(\boldsymbol{x},\boldsymbol{x}_{*})=\psi(\boldsymbol{x})^T\psi(\boldsymbol{x}_{*})$; finite landmark size and stabilization both affect this approximation. No approximation-error bound is assumed.}
